16,16,22,8,284,220,23,16,1124,15136,220,16,16,22,198,59,921,59,9640,59,37018,90,16,18,15170,24,19,22,22,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,18,15170,23,16,1124,15136,220,16,16,22,92,284,1124,37018,90,16,18,15170,23,16,1124,15136,220,16,16,22,532,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nGiven that the third term of a geometric sequence is $1053$ and the ninth term is $\\frac{13}{9}$, find the value of the seventh term.\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we start by recalling the general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term and \\(r\\) is the common ratio.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nUsing the general form, we can write:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\n\\]\n\nWe now have two equations:\n1. \\(ar^2 = 1053\\)\n2. \\(ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nTo find \\(r\\), we divide the second equation by the first equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053\n\\]\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{13}{9477} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117} = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"06ece1ee2a372ed83ecc465383dca27ffa3ef7207cb8d7365157611b8196f9f6"},"seconds":49.171424865722656,"seed":539161518989818840,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Write the expressions for the third and ninth terms in terms of the first term \\(a\\) and the common ratio \\(r\\).\n2. Use the given values to form equations.\n3. Solve these equations to find \\(a\\) and \\(r\\).\n4. Use the values of \\(a\\) and \\(r\\) to find the seventh term.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nThe general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\n\nSo, for the third term:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\nAnd for the ninth term:\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9} \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nWe can divide equation (2) by equation (1) to eliminate \\(a\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right side:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe need to simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(117 = 9 \\times 13\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 81 \\times 9 \\times 13 = 729 \\times 13\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{729 \\times 13} = \\frac{1}{729} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\nSo:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(r = \\frac{1}{3}\\) back into equation (1) to find \\(a\\):\n\\[\nar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\times 9 = 9477\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 9477 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 9477 \\times \\frac{1}{729} = \\frac{9477}{729} = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the seventh term of the geometric sequence, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Write the expressions for the third and ninth terms in terms of the first term \\(a\\) and the common ratio \\(r\\).\n2. Use the given values to form equations.\n3. Solve these equations to find \\(a\\) and \\(r\\).\n4. Use the values of \\(a\\) and \\(r\\) to find the seventh term.\n\nGiven:\n- The third term \\(a_3 = 1053\\)\n- The ninth term \\(a_9 = \\frac{13}{9}\\)\n\nThe general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of a geometric sequence is:\n\\[\na_n = ar^{n-1}\n\\]\n\nSo, for the third term:\n\\[\na_3 = ar^2 = 1053 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\nAnd for the ninth term:\n\\[\na_9 = ar^8 = \\frac{13}{9} \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nWe can divide equation (2) by equation (1) to eliminate \\(a\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{ar^8}{ar^2} = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{\\frac{13}{9}}{1053}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right side:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{9 \\times 1053} = \\frac{13}{9477}\n\\]\n\nWe need to simplify \\(\\frac{13}{9477}\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 9 \\times 1053 = 9 \\times (9 \\times 117) = 81 \\times 117\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{81 \\times 117}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(117 = 9 \\times 13\\):\n\\[\n9477 = 81 \\times 9 \\times 13 = 729 \\times 13\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\nr^6 = \\frac{13}{729 \\times 13} = \\frac{1}{729} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\nSo:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(r = \\frac{1}{3}\\) back into equation (1) to find \\(a\\):\n\\[\nar^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^2 = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na \\cdot \\frac{1}{9} = 1053\n\\]\n\\[\na = 1053 \\times 9 = 9477\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the seventh term \\(a_7\\):\n\\[\na_7 = ar^6\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 9477 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^6\n\\]\n\\[\na_7 = 9477 \\times \\frac{1}{729} = \\frac{9477}{729} = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the seventh term is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]","id":"algebra:753"}
